1
00:00:00,129 --> 00:00:06,010
Picture this, a company where every engineer has owner on every project.

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:08,500
Sure, deploying things is fast,

3
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:12,109
but so is accidentally deleting the things as well.

4
00:00:12,329 --> 00:00:15,640
So let's understand how we can do it in a most

5
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:17,629
appropriate Google recommended way.

6
00:00:17,799 --> 00:00:20,489
If we first talk about the IAM,

7
00:00:20,569 --> 00:00:25,780
IAM is Google's cloud system for deciding who can do what

8
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:29,024
and on which resource. It looks simple,

9
00:00:29,174 --> 00:00:32,764
but the complexity comes from understanding how those

10
00:00:32,764 --> 00:00:35,544
permissions flow through the resource hierarchy.

11
00:00:35,764 --> 00:00:40,154
We have majorly four types of identities users,

12
00:00:40,275 --> 00:00:44,555
usually from Google Workspace or a Cloud Identity, groups,

13
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:47,555
the recommended way to grant access to teams,

14
00:00:47,764 --> 00:00:48,595
service accounts,

15
00:00:48,724 --> 00:00:53,799
identities for application and automation, federated identities

16
00:00:53,799 --> 00:00:56,939
from systems like Active Directory or SAML.

17
00:00:57,130 --> 00:00:58,439
All these identities can receive roles.

18
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,340
Now we have the permissions.

19
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,480
Permissions are the smallest units.

20
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,239
Example could be like compute.instance.create.

21
00:01:09,410 --> 00:01:13,239
That gives someone the permission to create the instance.

22
00:01:13,599 --> 00:01:16,860
The rules are a collection of permissions.

23
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,705
There are three major categories for the roles.

24
00:01:19,785 --> 00:01:24,875
The first one is basic, which is like owner, editor, or a viewer.

25
00:01:25,125 --> 00:01:29,214
Google says these are the legacy and overly broad.

26
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:32,175
We avoid them in the enterprises.

27
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:35,824
Another one is the predefined roles, for example,

28
00:01:36,345 --> 00:01:39,665
like compute admin roles or some other kind of roles.

29
00:01:39,915 --> 00:01:41,415
These are the recommended.

30
00:01:41,635 --> 00:01:43,434
Another one is a custom role.

31
00:01:43,989 --> 00:01:47,830
You create them when predefined roles don't fit

32
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:51,949
completely. So IAM is overall identity, role,

33
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:57,569
permission, and the resource, but it is important to understand the hierarchy.

34
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:00,550
If you talk about the hierarchy at a higher level,

35
00:02:00,669 --> 00:02:02,529
the permission flows downward.

36
00:02:03,269 --> 00:02:07,599
For example, at the top level you have organization, then inside that folder,

37
00:02:07,849 --> 00:02:11,169
inside that projects, and inside that you have resource.

38
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:14,009
Let's understand it in a little more detail.

39
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:19,149
If we talk about the organization level roles, it applies everywhere.

40
00:02:19,449 --> 00:02:24,649
Whatever we have inside the organization, it comes down to all of them.

41
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:29,009
This is perfect for security admins, networking admins,

42
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:32,529
billing admins, and for logging and monitoring teams.

43
00:02:32,899 --> 00:02:36,309
Generally, these teams manage service globally.

44
00:02:36,529 --> 00:02:38,839
If you talk about the folder‑level roles,

45
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:42,529
let's say I assign the developer access to the development folder.

46
00:02:43,039 --> 00:02:47,324
Now every project inside the development automatically receives that permission.

47
00:02:47,485 --> 00:02:52,845
This is how the enterprise separates the production versus non‑production,

48
00:02:53,164 --> 00:02:59,085
internal versus external workloads, sensitive versus general use workloads.

49
00:02:59,244 --> 00:03:02,914
Project‑level roles apply to everything inside the project,

50
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:06,274
for example, like access to your virtual machines,

51
00:03:06,565 --> 00:03:11,580
your storage, databases, APIs, and many more.

52
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:16,789
This is typically where application teams receive their accesses.

53
00:03:17,050 --> 00:03:19,639
Finally, for a very specific use case,

54
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:26,660
you can assign roles directly to a resource like a single bucket or a VM.

55
00:03:26,919 --> 00:03:29,750
Now that we understand how IAM works,

56
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,830
let's talk about how enterprises actually use it.

57
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:39,350
Google recommends a top‑down, group‑based, and least privilege model.

58
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:41,779
Let's understand it in more detail.

59
00:03:41,979 --> 00:03:45,789
The top‑down approach means you assign the roles at

60
00:03:45,789 --> 00:03:47,729
the highest logical boundary.

61
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:48,850
For example,

62
00:03:49,149 --> 00:03:51,589
your network engineer group should have roles

63
00:03:51,589 --> 00:03:57,509
assigned at the organizational level, not individually across dozens of projects.

64
00:03:57,830 --> 00:04:01,970
So you give the security admins an organizational level,

65
00:04:02,389 --> 00:04:05,009
network admins, organizational folder level.

66
00:04:05,190 --> 00:04:06,869
Another pattern is a group based.

67
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:12,229
Instead of adding permission to the users one by one, you can add them to the

68
00:04:12,229 --> 00:04:15,570
Google Groups and then give the permission at the group level.

69
00:04:15,809 --> 00:04:19,730
For example, you could have a group for developer application 1,

70
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:24,279
sre‑team, networking‑team, and similarly for other teams.

71
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:26,829
Another pattern would be the list privileges.

72
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:30,750
Every identity gets exactly the access it needs.

73
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:33,339
No more, no less.

74
00:04:33,459 --> 00:04:36,929
So no one gets the owner and no one gets the editor.

75
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:39,880
Give exact permission as needed.

76
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:40,779
For example,

77
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:44,900
if developer needs only permission to view the logs, you

78
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:46,839
should get the logging viewer access.

79
00:04:47,049 --> 00:04:49,779
Another pattern is separation of duties.

80
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:52,440
Enterprises follow a four i's principle.

81
00:04:52,579 --> 00:04:55,239
Not everyone should be able to do everything.

82
00:04:55,459 --> 00:04:58,059
For example, dev deploy the apps.

83
00:04:58,179 --> 00:05:00,250
SRE manages the infrastructure,

84
00:05:00,549 --> 00:05:04,959
security reviews the policies, and networking teams manage the VPCs,

85
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:09,079
and I make these boundaries enforceable and auditable.

86
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:11,429
Another pattern is a service account.

87
00:05:11,649 --> 00:05:15,970
As first class citizens, service accounts power the automation.

88
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:20,760
They can be useful for CI/CD pipelines, for Terraform,

89
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,880
and for many other use cases,

90
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:27,149
although it is recommended that using the workload identity

91
00:05:27,429 --> 00:05:30,929
wherever possible instead of using the service keys.
